3. Results and discussion
3.1. Adsorption of single O atom on the SurfCD
We first analyzed the adsorption of O2 molecule on the SurfCD. The optimized configurations as shown in Supplementary Fig. S1, O2 molecule spontaneously decomposed into two O atoms adsorbed on different sites on all SurfCD. This result indicates that the configuration of O2 molecule adsorbed on the SurfCD is unstable and relaxation eventually leads to the dissociation of the O2 molecule, which is consistent with the finding that O2 molecule adsorbed on the clean 4H-SiC surface [26,49]. Five different types of adsorption sites were considered to investigate the most stable adsorption sites of single O atom on the SurfCD, including the top site of surface Si atom (T), bridge site between two Si atoms on the surface (B), fcc hollow site above the surface SiC hexagon (F), hcp hollow site above the surface SiC hexagon (H), other sites around C
related point defects (O). As shown in Fig. 2(a–c), these O adsorption sites on the SurfCi, SurfCsi and SurfVc were marked with dashed circles of different colors. The adsorption of the O atom on SurfCD is more complex than on clean surface because the C-related point defects decrease high symmetry adsorption sites of the O atom on the surface. For the adsorption of single O atom on the SurfCi, SurfCsi and SurfVc, we considered 28, 14 and 15 possible configurations, respectively. After optimization, all the stable configurations are depicted in Supplementary Figs. S2, S3 and S4. Almost all F and H sites of the initial configurations were transformed to B sites, illustrating that the F and H adsorption sites of single O atom on the SurfCi, SurfCsi and SurfVc were unstable. It is inconsistent with the existence of four possible stable adsorption sites (T, B, F and H) of single O atom on the clean 4H-SiC (0001) surface [16,50], which may be related to the variation of atomic distribution and lattice strain on the local surface caused by Crelated point defects. This is due to that, there is difference between the atomic arrangement of the surface with defects and that of the next substrate surface, which causes the strain in the near-surface region, further generating lateral and normal forces on the surface; while the adsorption of O atom on the surface will also induce local lateral and normal forces, the two forces on the surface interact with each other [51,52]. The distribution of Eads of single O atom at different sites calculated according to Eq. (1) is shown in Fig. 3(a–c). In the same system, the lower the Eads represents the stronger interaction between the adsorbed atoms and the surface; the lower the Eads, the more stable the configuration is. For SurfCi, SurfCsi and SurfVc, the lowest Eads of a single O atom were 4.133, 4.228 and 4.781 eV, respectively. Compared with the clean 4H-SiC (0001) surface ( 4.432 eV), the Eads of single O atom on the SurfCi and SurfCsi were higher, and that on the SurfVc was lower, which indicates the existence of VC is beneficial to the adsorption of single O atom on the 4H-SiC surface. In contrast, the adsorptions of O atom on the SurfCi and SurfCsi were energetically less favorable compared with clean surface. The difference of C-related point defects on adsorption energy may cause different oxidation rates at the initial oxidation, further affecting the thickness uniformity of the oxide film. In addition, the most stable adsorption site of single O atom on these SurfCD was the B site (Fig. 3(d–f)), consistent with the type of the most stable adsorption site on the clean 4H-SiC surface [16]. The difference is that the most stable B site on SurfCD is located between the Si atoms connected to the C-related point defects.
3.2. Adsorptions of O atoms on the SurfCD with different coverages
As the oxidation process continued, more O atoms were introduced into the 4H-SiC surface. The increase of O coverages affected the configuration and energy of O adsorption on the surface, and the type and structure of surface defects were also changed. In this section, we studied the adsorption of O atoms on the SurfCi, SurfCsi and SurfVc with O coverage ranging from 1/9 to 1 ML. After sampling numerous configurations, we selected the lowest-energy configurations under different O coverage as shown in Figs. 4, 5 and 6, respectively. Fig. 4(a–i) displays the lowest-energy O adsorption configurations on SurfCi for O coverage from 1/9 to 1 ML. Our previous work has proved that the optimized C interstitial defect was a composite defect composed of C antisite (CSi) and Si adatom, the Si adatom connected with CSi and two Si atoms adjacent to CSi through Si-C and Si-Si bonds, respectively [35]. As observed in Fig. 4(a) and (b), the most stable structure with the O coverage of 1/9 and 2/9 ML were the Si-O-Si structures formed by that the introduced O atoms destroyed the two Si-Si bonds. For the O coverage between 1/3 to 5/9, the most favorable absorption sites of O atoms were on the Si atoms with dangling bond (Fig. 4(c–e)). For the O coverage of 5/9 ML, all Si dangling bonds on the surface were saturated by O atoms, and the Si-C bond between the surface and subsurface SiC bilayer was broken, resulting in the generation of Si dangling bonds on the subsurface bilayer. With O coverage increasing up to 2/3 ML, the Si
