C bond between CSi and Si adatom was broken, the surface C interstitial defect was transformed into CSi defect and O-Si-O structure absorbed on the surface, the introduced O atom was preferentially absorbed on the top site of the Si atom in the O-Si-O structure (Fig. 4(f)). When the O coverage reaches 7/9 ML, the SiO4 tetrahedral structure with Si adatom as the center was obtained on the surface. The average Si-O bond length and O-Si-O bond angle in the SiO4 tetrahedral were 1.67 Å and 116.24◦, which are close to the structural parameters of SiO2 measured in the experiment [53,54]. Thus, the SiO4 structure may be the initial nucleation precursor of thermally oxidized SiO2 on the SurfCi (Fig. 4(g)). When the O coverage was increased to 8/9 or 1 ML, the introduced O atom tended to destroy the Si-C bond on the surface SiC bilayer, forming the Si-O-C structures on the surface (Fig. 4(h-i)). In addition, we considered the surface CO emission for O coverage above 5/9 ML. The results showed that the stable structure of CO emission existed only when the O coverage was 1 ML (Supplementary Fig. S5(a)), but the formation energy of this stable structure lay 1.197 eV above that of the structure shown in Fig. 4(i). We propose that the CO emission are energetically less favorable when the O coverage is below 1 ML on the SurfCi.
Fig. 5(a–i) displays the lowest-energy O adsorption configurations on SurfCsi for O coverage from 1/9 to 1 ML. The surface CSi defect was formed by the C atom substituting the Si atom [35]. As depicted in Fig. 5 (a–c), the preferential adsorption sites of the introduced O atoms were the B sites between Si atoms adjacent to CSi for the O coverage from 1/9 to 1/3 ML. At 4/9 ML, the Si dangling bonds on the surface were saturated by O atoms (Fig. 5(d)). When the O coverage reached 5/9 ML, the O atoms were prone to destroy the Si-C bonds on the surface, and the SiO-C structure containing an unsaturated C atom was formed (Fig. 5(e)). As the O coverage increasing, more Si-C bonds were broken to generate more unsaturated C atoms, these unsaturated C atoms combined to form C-cluster defects, such as C2-, C5- and C6-clusters depicted in Fig. 5(f–i). With O coverage increasing up to 1 ML, the O atom diffused to the subsurface, and the Si-O-Si structure was obtained between bilayers. Furthermore, the stable structures of CO emission existed at 2/3 ML and 7/9 ML O coverage (Supplementary Fig. S5(b) and (c)), and the corresponding formation energies of these stable structures were 3.76 eV and 2.21 eV higher than that of the structure shown in Fig. 5(f) and (g) respectively. This result indicates that CO emission is less likely to occur at the initial oxidation for SurfCsi, the O atoms tend to absorb on the surface and diffuse to the subsurface. Fig. 6(a–i) displays the lowest-energy O adsorption configurations on SurfVc for O coverage from 1/9 to 1 ML. Due to the existence of C vacancy, SurfVc had more Si dangling bonds than the clean 4H-SiC surface, resulting in the introduced O atoms were more preferentially bonded with the surface Si atoms, which is consistent with the lowest Eads of single O atom on the SurfVc calculated in section 3.1. For O coverage of 1/9 ML, Fig. 6(a) and Supplementary Fig. S6(a) show that the O atom preferentially bands with the Si atoms around the C vacancy, and accompanied by the formation of Si-Si bond between bilayers. When the O coverage reached 1/3 ML, the Si-C bond between bilayers was broken
(Fig. 6(c)). As shown in Supplementary Fig. S6(b), the stretching effect of O and Si atoms on Si1, Si2 and Si3 atoms around C1 atom caused the upward movement of C1 atom, breaking the Si-C between C1 and Si4 atoms (marked by dashed line). As the O coverage increased to 2/3 ML, most of the O atoms were bonded with the Si atoms around C vacancy (Fig. 6(f)), causing large local surface strain and forming the C–C defect as displayed in Supplementary Fig. S6(c). When the O coverage reached 7/9 ML, the Si-O-Si structure was formed by the diffusion of the introduced O atom to the subsurface, as shown in Fig. 6(g). All the Si dangling bonds were saturated at O coverage of 8/9 ML (Fig. 6(h)), which requires more O atoms than that of clean surface. When the O coverage reached 1 ML, the Si-C bonds were broken by O atoms to form Si-O-C structures (Fig. 6(i)). Moreover, the formation energy of the stable configuration of CO emission (Supplementary Fig. S5(d)) was 1.913 eV more than that of the structure depicted in Fig. 6(i). Analogous to SurfCi and SurfCsi, the emission of CO is almost impossible on the SurfVc for the O coverage below 1 ML. Fig. 7 shows the Eads of O atoms as the function of the O coverage on SurfCi, SurfCsi and SurfVc. The Eads of O atoms under different coverage was negative, indicating that the oxidation of SurfCD was spontaneous, especially for SurfVc. The Eads increased with the O coverage, which indicates that the repulsive interaction of O atoms built up with the number of O atoms increasing. Another effect of surface C-related point defects on the initial oxidation of 4H-SiC was found in the variation of bond length at the SiO2/SiC interface. Fig. 8(a) and (b) show the distribution of Si-O and SiC bonds extracted from the surface models displayed in Figs. 4(i), 5(i) and 6(i). The average Si-O bond lengths on the SurfCi, SurfCsi and SurfVc (1.67, 1.70 and 1.69 Å, respectively) were larger than that in clean 4HSiC surface (1.63 Å) and bulk α-SiO2 (1.62 Å) [27,55]. The average Si-C bond lengths on the SurfCi, SurfCsi and SurfVc (1.94, 1.92 and 1.94 Å, respectively) were larger than that in clean 4H-SiC surface and bulk 4HSiC (1.89 Å) [55,56]. The more considerable average Si-O and Si-C bond lengths on the SurfCD may be attributed to the local lattice strain of the surfaces caused by C-related point defects, which is similar to the increase of Ga-N bond lengths at the GaN/GaAs interface caused by that the eight relaxed N atoms induced strain along z direction [57]. On the other hand, the increased repulsive interaction of O atoms with the number of surface atoms may also cause the lengthening of the Si-O and Si-C bonds. From the above analysis and comparison, surface C-related point defects can be seen as a critical factor affecting the initial oxidation of 4H-SiC, which determine the adsorption sites of O atoms, the diffusion of O atoms to 4H-SiC and the interfacial lattice strain. The O atoms combined with the Si dangling bonds on the surface at low O coverage, preferentially adsorbed on the B site of two unoccupied Si atoms adjacent to the C-related defects. When the O coverage reached 5/9, 4/9 and 8/9 ML, all Si dangling bonded on the SurfCi, SurfCsi and SurfVc were saturated. Compared with the clean 4H-SiC surface (1/2 ML) [16], more O atoms were required for saturating Si dangling bonds on these SurfCD except SurfCsi, which is directly related to the number of available Si
