3.3. Thermodynamic equilibrium diagrams for initial oxidation of SurfCD
This section investigated the variations of surface stability with temperature and pressure for O/SurfCi, O/SurfCsi and O/SurfVc. Firstly, the obtained relative surface free energies (Δγ) by Eq. (4) as a function of μO were depicted in Fig. 9. The range of μO to ensure the stability of SiO2 was 9.16 eV ≤ μO ≤ 4.92 eV, which is represented by the area between two vertical dashed lines in Fig. 9. The slopes of Δγ ~ μO curves increased with the of O coverage, consistent with the findings of clean surface [16]. The more negative the surface free energy is, the more stable the structure is [16,43]. For O-poor, the structure with low O coverage is more stable; on the contrary, the structure with high O coverage is more stable for O-rich. This is consistent with the variation of structural stability of different O coverage in GaN/HfO2 system [58]. Fig. 9(a) shows the variation curves of Δγ with μO for the O/SurfCi structure under different O coverage. The Δγ of SurfCi is lowest for μO < 9.21 eV, indicating the SurfCi was the most stable. For 9.21 eV < μO < 9.16 eV, the Δγ of the structure with 4/9 ML O coverage is lowest,
corresponding the structure with 4/9 ML O coverage became the most favorable. For μO > 9.16 eV, the most stable structure transited from 4/ 9 ML to the bulk SiO2, indicating that SiO2 begins to nucleate when the O coverage on the SurfCi reaches 4/9 ML or higher, which is consistent with the fact aforementioned in section 3.2 that the SiO4 tetrahedron structure formed on the SurfCi may be the initial nucleation precursor of thermally oxidized SiO2. In addition, some possible intermediate configurations existed during the formation of bulk SiO2, such as the structure with 5/9 ML, 7/9 ML, 8/9 ML and 1 ML, corresponding μO values of phase transition were 8.91, 8.65, 8.02 and 7.17 eV, respectively. These configurations were more stable than 4/9 ML but less stable than SiO2. Fig. 9(b) shows the variation curves of Δγ with μO for the O/SurfCsi structure under different O coverage. The SurfCsi was most stable for μO < 9.21 eV, while for 9.21 eV < μO < 9.16 eV, the structure with 1/3 ML O coverage became the most stable. For μO > 9.16 eV, the most stable structure was transformed to bulk SiO2. Notably, the transition process from the SurfCsi to SiO2 was almost aligned with that of the
