4. Conclusion
The DFT calculations in this work have been performed to provide deep insights into the initial oxidation on the 4H-SiC (0001) surface with C-related point defects. We have found that the most stable adsorption sites of single O atom were bridge sites between the Si atoms connected to the C-related point defects on these SurfCD. The adsorption energy of single O atom on SurfVc was the lowest among these SurfCD and lower than that on clean surface. As the oxidation process continued, the O atoms preferentially combined with the Si dangling bonds on the surface at low O coverage, and more O atoms were required for saturating Si dangling bonds on these SurfCD except SurfCsi. The diffusion rates of O atoms on all SurfCD were slower than that on the clean surface. C-related point defects could also cause more significant lattice strain and distortion on the surface, which increases the possibility of forming more interface defects during oxidation. Furthermore, the thermodynamic calculations showed that the structures under low O coverage on the SurfCi and SurfCsi were unstable before the formation of SiO2. Some intermediate structures with defects were observed during the formation of SiO2 on these SurfCD. The newly generated interface Si dangling bonds, Si-O-C structure and C-clusters, introducing charge states near CBM and VBM of 4H-SiC, which may further affect the voltage stability of 4H-SiC MOS devices. The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.
